{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794856351,"nonce":"mu/P9rwngXZGakFj7nnlQpPDULE=","title":"A program's blocking wait does not serve its ash_host_watch handlers unless fibers are alive","message":"Reported by hxn (hxn-bd session, 2026-11-16). `Future.await` in hxn-gpu loops on a native `wait(20)`\n(blocks in memory.atomic.wait32) and `Sys.sleep(0.001)`, and the future it awaits is settled by an\n`ash_host_watch` handler. It never settles under Ash.\n\nFrom the source (std/src/fiber.rs, rt.rs), not yet reproduced here:\n- Watch handlers run only where the runtime polls them: `park` of the program's main logical thread\n  (every pass of its loop), `host_wait` and the host program's `turn`.\n- `Sys.sleep` is `rt::sleep_ns`. With fibers alive it parks on a scheduler timer, which polls the watches.\n  With none it is `std::thread::sleep`, which does not.\n- A native blocking wait in an HDLL never returns to the runtime.\nSo an await built from a native wait and `Sys.sleep` in a program that has started no Haxe thread polls\nnothing. Waiting on a `sys.thread.Lock` that the handler releases does work, because the main thread's\n`Lock.wait` is a `park`.\n\nOptions for the runtime:\n- the no-fiber branch of `sleep_ns` sleeps in slices of at most a millisecond and polls the watches while\n  any are registered;\n- an exported `ash_host_poll` that runs the due handlers on the calling (program) thread, for a library's\n  own wait loop.\nEither way, document in docs/wasm/host-abi.md which waits serve a watch (see the earlier ash_host_watch issue).","files":null}]}